  "Question": "Find the smallest positive number from the numbers below",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(10-3\\sqrt{11}\\)",
    "B": "\\(3\\sqrt{11}-10\\)",
    "C": "\\(18-5\\sqrt{13}\\)",
    "D": "\\(51-10\\sqrt{26}\\)",
    "E": "\\(10\\sqrt{26}-51\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "First determine which choices are positive. We can compare the positive terms in each difference by comparing their squares:\n\\[\n10^2=100>99=(3\\sqrt{11})^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n18^2=324<325=(5\\sqrt{13})^2,\n\\]\n\\[\n51^2=2601>2600=(10\\sqrt{26})^2.\n\\]\nThus A and D are positive, while B, C, and E are negative.\n\nTo compare A and D exactly, notice that the difference of squares is 1 in both cases:\n\\[\n(10-3\\sqrt{11})(10+3\\sqrt{11})=1,\n\\]\n\\[\n(51-10\\sqrt{26})(51+10\\sqrt{26})=1.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n10-3\\sqrt{11}=\\frac1{10+3\\sqrt{11}},\n\\qquad\n51-10\\sqrt{26}=\\frac1{51+10\\sqrt{26}}.\n\\]\nBoth denominators are positive. Moreover, \\(10+3\\sqrt{11}<20\\), whereas \\(51+10\\sqrt{26}>51\\). The larger positive denominator gives the smaller reciprocal. Therefore the smallest positive choice is \\(51-10\\sqrt{26}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
